{"type":"after","callId":"vrtg@2118","endTime":46281.146,"result":{"value":"Fetch test contributor brief\nBrief\n\nThe Fetch Standard’s tests are kept in the fetch/ directory of web-platform-tests/wpt and can be seen at https://wpt.fyi/results/fetch. [1] [2]\n\nExact source question\n\nWhere are the Fetch Standard’s tests kept, and where can I see them running against browser engines? Cite the README statements supporting both.\n\nYour note — supplied by you, not a source claim\n\nPersonal handoff for my next Fetch test contribution. These are my own proposed actions and working notes, not instructions or verified claims from the source.\n\nBefore changing anything, I will record the browser and version, a minimal public example, the outcome I expected and what I actually saw. I will remove cookies, authorization headers, account details and private URLs from anything I share.\n\n1. Reopen this brief and follow its original-source link. Compare the saved retrieval time with the current README. If the evidence changed, ask a new focused question instead of editing the accepted copy.\n2. Open the browser-results location from the brief. Write down the engine and platform I inspected. A saved answer is a pointer to evidence, not proof that a bug exists or a test passes.\n3. Search the cited test directory for a related case before proposing another test. Keep the candidate filename, my reason for choosing it and any unresolved question in my own draft.\n4. Separate reproduction from implementation. First describe a small public fixture and expected behavior; then explain the proposed test and the observation it should distinguish.\n5. Read the project's current contribution and review instructions before submitting anything. I have not checked those instructions yet, and this brief does not establish them.\n6. Keep a handoff log of what I tried, which evidence I saved and what remains uncertain. After interruption, inspect saved work and continue deliberately rather than repeating accepted research.\n7. Preserve the PDF and HTML with their recorded hashes and original citations. Ask for missing guidance instead of guessing. My personal checklist is not a source-backed conclusion.\n\nReference [1] · Literal saved-source quote (source-1, text 1914–2055): Tests for the Fetch Standard can be found in the `fetch/` directory of [`web-platform-tests/wpt`](https://github.com/web-platform-tests/wpt).\n\nReference [2] · Literal saved-source quote (source-1, text 2057–2189): A dashboard showing the tests running against browser engines can be seen at [wpt.fyi/results/fetch](https://wpt.fyi/results/fetch).\n\nSaved source source-1\n\nREADME.md\n\nURL: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/whatwg/fetch/main/README.md\n\nRetrieved: 2026-10-29T22:06:27.711Z\n\nSnapshot: 52d48f68-3ab1-467d-bbd4-f5467e4b41ef\n\nResponse SHA-256: d484144421db3f758ef98fe5bf13c3dbe1e07a357033af5288298ea0f4f2ceaa\n\nText SHA-256: d484144421db3f758ef98fe5bf13c3dbe1e07a357033af5288298ea0f4f2ceaa\n\nScope and provenance\n\nThe model selected citation span IDs; the host copied their exact saved text. This checks text identity, not independent factual answer accuracy.\n\nWritten by the local model from the accepted answer and selected saved passages. Fixed host code rendered this document. Source claims and general factual accuracy were not independently verified.\n\nParent question: 7c31d3ef-f1c3-4094-85b6-7bab80ca81e7\n\nBrief goal: a09643e1-9a33-4d6d-b6d5-dc5e87d516bd"}}
